file: let If-None-Match override If-Modified-Since

RFC 9110 13.2.2 evaluates If-None-Match before If-Modified-Since, and a
recipient must ignore If-Modified-Since when the request carries
If-None-Match. uh_file_data() tests If-Modified-Since first, so a GET
whose date is at or after the file's mtime gets 304 Not Modified even
when its entity tag does not match the file.

A browser revalidates with both validators of its cached copy. When that
copy is newer than the file now served, the date passes and the tag does
not: after a downgrade to an older image, or when another device served
the same address before. The browser then keeps the stale file and has
no way to learn that it changed.

With If-None-Match present, If-Modified-Since is skipped and the entity
tag decides: a match still gets 304, a mismatch gets 200 and the file.
A request with only If-Modified-Since is answered as before. A request
that also carries If-Match, If-Range or If-Unmodified-Since reaches
those checks now that the date is skipped, so a failed precondition
answers 412 where the date used to answer 304.

diff --git a/file.c b/file.c
index 23b7671..f83e50d 100644
--- a/file.c
+++ b/file.c
@@ -450,7 +450,8 @@ static int uh_file_if_modified_since(struct client *cl, struct stat *s)
 	char *hdr = uh_file_header(cl, HDR_IF_MODIFIED_SINCE);
 	time_t t;
 
-	if (!hdr)
+	/* RFC 9110 13.2.2: ignored when If-None-Match is present */
+	if (!hdr || uh_file_header(cl, HDR_IF_NONE_MATCH))
 		return true;
 
 	t = uh_file_date2unix(hdr);
